# The public half of the Arbos update signing key.
#
# Every published payload — the macOS app zip, the Linux tarball — is signed
# with the private half, which lives only in the repository secret
# ARBOS_UPDATE_SIGNING_KEY and is never written to a file that can be
# committed. This half is compiled into the app: an update installs only if
# its bytes verify against the line below.
#
# Nothing here is Apple's. Notarization is a separate promise made to
# Gatekeeper about the first launch of a downloaded app; this is the promise
# made to the running app about the payload it is replacing itself with. When
# the Developer ID certificate arrives, the payload gains a notarized
# signature and keeps this one.
#
# To set it up once, from a checkout:
#
#   cargo run -q -p arbos-update --bin arbos-updatectl -- keygen \
#       --public crates/arbos-update/update-key.pub | gh secret set ARBOS_UPDATE_SIGNING_KEY
#
# That writes this file (commit it) and pipes the secret straight into the
# repository secret without it ever reaching a terminal or a shell history.
#
# Until the line exists, a build refuses every update rather than trusting an
# unsigned one, and the publish workflow skips itself with a note.
ed25519 tRMVEMFE8nHJfhoFBIt3pIjEUEFiqvXB9HiB8Rnn3YQ=
